Popper held that corroboration is not probability: a theory that has survived severe tests is not thereby made more probable. Yet working scientists, and Popper himself when choosing which theory to act on, prefer the better-corroborated theory. Is that preference defensible on Popper's own terms, or does it smuggle back in the induction he rejected? Make the strongest case for each answer, then say which one survives and what accepting it costs.

## target-conjecture
TARGET CONJECTURE 66df759e09c49faedd7f1b9e7ebf1d8ceeac2fed60fcd2cbf4ca3d2592289264
The preference is justified not by looking backward at survival rates but forward at informational content and verisimilitude (truth-likeness). Severe tests target high-content theories; surviving them suggests the theory captures more structural reality than a refuted rival, even if we cannot assign a probability to this capture. We choose the better-corroborated theory because it is our best current guess at being closer to the truth, not because it is statistically safer. This costs us the ability to quantify 'closeness to truth' precisely, forcing science to remain a qualitative journey toward an unreachable horizon.

